\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

Our findings are bounded by three scope constraints. First, the model sample comprises eight open-weight 7--9B parameter models; the results do not generalize to API-based models, larger models, or fine-tuned variants. Second, all experiments run on single-GPU inference (NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000); multi-GPU tensor parallelism introduces additional numerical non-associativity that our design does not capture. Third, the benchmark sample covers three multiple-choice and two free-form tasks; other evaluation formats such as code generation, long-form text production, and multi-turn dialogue may exhibit different variance structures.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Variance Decomposition in ML/NLP Evaluation.}
Factorial decomposition of evaluation variance has roots in the ML reproducibility literature. \citet{bouthillier2021accounting} introduced an ANOVA-based template that decomposes benchmark variance across implementation details, hyperparameters, and data splits, establishing that design choices account for a substantial share of reproducibility failures. Several subsequent studies applied similar decomposition logic to specific facets of model evaluation \citep{dodge2020finetuning, henderson2018deep, magnusson2023reproducibility}. \citet{mesting2026hidden} brought Generalizability theory into LLM evaluation, decomposing variance across five methodology facets on a single benchmark; judge identity dominated at 43.8\%, with prompt$\times$judge interaction at 12.4\%. However, this study used a single benchmark, included no infrastructure facets such as numerical precision, and fixed the judge as an LLM evaluator rather than isolating other sources. \citet{kunievsky2025intent} proposed an alternative decomposition that separates intent uncertainty, articulation variance, and model uncertainty, but this framework does not cross infrastructure and methodology facets in a factorial design. Our work extends these approaches by crossing seven facets across five benchmarks, enabling direct comparison of variance structures across evaluation formats.

\paragraph{Infrastructure and Prompt Variance.}
A parallel line of work has examined individual variance sources in isolation. On the infrastructure side, \citet{yuan2025layercast} documented up to 9\% accuracy variation from numerical precision and GPU type but provided no factorial attribution. \citet{messina2026background} formalized the concept of background temperature from floating-point non-associativity in GPU computation, and \citet{thinkingmachines2025batch} proposed batch-invariant kernels to mitigate CUDA nondeterminism. On the methodology side, \citet{sclar2024formatting} found up to 76\% accuracy swings from prompt formatting changes on fixed hardware, and BrittleBench \citep{brittlebench2026} reported 50\% variance from prompt perturbation. \citet{pezeshkpour2024ordering} showed that answer ordering in multiple-choice benchmarks introduces systematic bias, while \citet{bui2025seeds} and \citet{biderman2024lessons} documented sensitivity to random seeds and training configurations. Each of these studies isolates one factor while holding others fixed, which precludes estimating interactions between sources or comparing their relative magnitudes within a unified design.

\paragraph{IRT and Efficient Benchmarking.}
Item Response Theory (IRT) offers a complementary perspective on evaluation design by modeling item difficulty and discrimination parameters. \citet{lalor2019irt} introduced IRT-based item analysis for NLP benchmarks, and \citet{rodriguez2021leaderboards} showed that IRT reveals hidden properties of evaluation items that raw accuracy scores obscure. Building on this foundation, \citet{polo2024tinybenchmarks} developed tinyBenchmarks, using IRT-based subsampling to reduce evaluation cost while preserving ranking accuracy, and \citet{zhou2026irt} recommended a 116-item subset via PSN-IRT for efficient MMLU evaluation. \citet{perlitz2023efficient} explored related strategies for benchmark compression. These approaches optimize item selection for latent trait estimation but do not model variance from prompt, seed, or ordering facets. IRT-selected items evaluated under a single methodological condition can therefore systematically underestimate the number of items required for reliable measurement across diverse evaluation settings.

\paragraph{Binary Data and Psychometrics Foundations.}
Our application of G-theory to binary correctness scores draws on established psychometric methodology. \citet{shavelson1991generalizability} and \citet{brennan2001generalizability} provide the canonical framework for multi-facet reliability analysis. \citet{mushquash2006gtheory} extended G-theory to binary data, and \citet{jiang2018bayesian} developed logistic-link generalized G-theory for binary outcomes. \citet{tuerlinckx2006glmm} established the connection between generalized linear mixed models and G-theory variance components. These methods ground our use of Henderson Method~I for binary correctness scores (\S\ref{sec:binary}).

\paragraph{Positioning.}
Relative to \citet{mesting2026hidden}, we fix the judge (generation-based scoring) to isolate the remaining facets; their judge-dominated variance (43.8\%) would otherwise mask all other sources. Our multi-benchmark design reveals format-conditional structure that single-benchmark studies cannot detect. Relative to IRT approaches \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks, zhou2026irt}, G-theory and IRT optimize orthogonal dimensions of evaluation design: IRT selects informative items, while G-theory determines the conditions needed for a target reliability. Our D-study quantifies this complementarity: multi-facet designs require 10.0$\times$ fewer items than single-condition protocols for equivalent reliability. Relative to isolated-factor studies \citep{yuan2025layercast, sclar2024formatting, brittlebench2026}, our design crosses infrastructure and methodology facets within a single factorial framework, enabling interaction estimation that per-factor studies cannot provide.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This study establishes that the variance structure of LLM evaluation is format-conditional. Across three multiple-choice benchmarks, item selection accounts for 34--38\% of total variance, while on free-form benchmarks (GSM8K, MATH), model, prompt, and item each contribute 12--24\% with no single dominant source. Model$\times$item interaction (up to 33\%) is the hidden second-largest source: models differ in which items they find difficult, not in overall ability. Our cross-benchmark D-study framework extends the single-benchmark G-theory approach of \citet{mesting2026hidden} to format-conditional comparison and actionable design budgets.

The format-conditional structure implies distinct optimization strategies. For multiple-choice benchmarks, item pool composition is the primary reliability lever; varying prompts or seeds contributes negligible variance. For free-form benchmarks, prompt design and model selection matter as much as item selection, and evaluation protocols should systematically vary both. Across formats, multi-facet designs are 10.0$\times$ more efficient than single-condition designs (55 versus 552 items for $G \geq 0.80$). BF16 and FP16 are interchangeable under binary scoring on single-GPU inference, which eliminates precision as a required control variable. The released D-study calculator enables practitioners to determine the number of items, models, and conditions needed for a target reliability before data collection.

Two extensions are planned. Multi-GPU tensor parallelism will test whether precision separability holds under distributed numerical non-associativity. Fine-tuning variance analysis will quantify how adaptation reshapes the item difficulty profiles that drive model$\times$item interaction.

